    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00005",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00294:title:sect-00005-title",
        "text": "Reductive Amination of Aldehydes and Ketones"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00010",
          "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00010"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "Phenyl-2-propanone reacts with ammonia, hydrogen, nickel or sodium borohydride to form amphetamine and water."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00011",
          "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00011"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00294:caption:fig-00008-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "A three-step mechanism shows the reductive amination of a ketone using sodium borohydride or hydrogen, nickel to form an amine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00012",
          "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00012"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "A carbonyl compound reacts with ammonia and sodium borohydride to form a primary amine. It undergoes two reaction with primary and secondary amine to form secondary and tertiary amine, respectively."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00013",
          "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00013"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "Glutamate-5-semialdehyde releases water to form 1-pyrrolinium-5-carboxylate. This reacts to form proline as nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide hydrogen converts to nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide cation."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "id": "exam-00001",
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00294:example-title:exam-00001-title",
            "text": "Using a Reductive Amination Reaction"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00014",
              "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00014"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00015",
              "segmentId": "m00294:para:para-00015",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00294:para-title:para-00015-title",
                "text": "Strategy"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00016",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00294:para-title:para-00016-title",
                "text": "Solution"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00011",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00011-alt",
              "text": "The structure of N-methyl-2-phenylethylamine. It is a benzene ring with the substituent C H 2 C H 2 N H C H 3."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00294:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "2-Phenylacetaldehyde reacts with methylamine in the presence of sodium borohydride to form N-methyl-2-phenylethanamine. The product is also formed by the reaction of 2-phenylethanamine and formaldehyde with sodium borohydride."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_06_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
